# Line endings are content here, not formatting.
#
# `frontend/vendor/wordlist/en.txt` is read by the documents editor's spell
# checker as one word per line and matched with `Set.has`. Git on Windows
# checks text files out with CRLF by default, which turns every entry into
# "offline\r" and makes all 92,972 of them miss: reported as "litterally
# everything isnt in the dictionary", with 268 suggestions on a 298-word
# document. The loader trims each line too, which fixes copies that already
# exist; this stops the checkout from breaking it in the first place.
#
# The same reasoning covers the rest of the frontend: a `?v=` cache stamp, a
# CSS brace count and a byte length in a test all change under CRLF.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Except the Windows launchers, where LF is not a formatting preference but a
# bug. cmd.exe does not parse a batch file: it seeks a label by byte offset
# and re-reads the file at each `call`, and its line scanner expects CRLF. In
# an LF-only file every `call :label` lands mid-line, and the interpreter
# prints "The system cannot find the batch label specified - <label>" and
# carries on with the *next* command as though the call had returned.
#
# Reported from a real install: "[1/4] Using the app's virtual environment. /
# The system cannot find the batch label specified - bail_if_cancelled / [2/4]
# Dependencies already up to date". `bail_if_cancelled` is what stops the
# installer after someone answers no, so on that machine every cancel was
# silently ignored and setup ran on regardless.
#
# `eol=crlf` rather than `-text`: the files stay text to git (diffable,
# mergeable, and the lone-LF lint below reads them as text), and the checkout
# writes the line endings the interpreter needs on every platform, so a
# release built on Linux ships a launcher that works.
# `tests/test_launcher_scripts.py::test_the_windows_launchers_use_crlf` fails
# if one ever comes back with lone LFs.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.webp binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
